   "text": "2. Money with strings: loans and aid made conditional on honest government",
   "level": 1
  },
  {
   "kind": "prose",
   "text": "Four cases, 1922 to 1961, in which the United States tied money to a clean-government program: a bank loan to Cuba, aid to Greece, and aid to a military government in Korea. The thread is the lever: officials wrote that aid or consent to a loan could be given or withheld depending on action against corruption. The Cuban cases also show the lever's limit, since the amnesty bill the Department opposed became law in 1924."
  },
